        ":question/id": "cb7196e5-d7f5-57c6-8585-8f9e64cfa8a4",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4 x - 1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x - 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x - 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 3 \\\\ u & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4 x - 1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\ln(u)|_{3}^{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln(11)-\\ln(3)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}{4} + \\frac{\\log{\\left(11 \\right)}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
