        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{x + 12}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x + 12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8\\,\\text{d}u & =8\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x + 12.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{x + 12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u & =8\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\ln(u)|_{12}^{13} \\\\  & =8(\\ln(13)-\\ln(12)) \\\\  & =- 8 \\log{\\left(12 \\right)} + 8 \\log{\\left(13 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
