        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2}{2 x + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 1\\,\\text{d}u & =2\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2 x + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2}{2 x + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u & =1\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =1\\ln(u)|_{7}^{9} \\\\  & =1(\\ln(9)-\\ln(7)) \\\\  & =- \\log{\\left(7 \\right)} + 2 \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
